Article 11

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 10, is carried out by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of: a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental and spiritual; and g. peace and order.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in an integrated and sustainable manner.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation and protection of women and children from victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. humanity;
b. respect for the rights of victims;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. protection;
e. non-discrimination; andf. best interests of the victim.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 4

Women and children who are victims of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings; b. the right to health and psychological services from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to legal assistance;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidential handling;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to complaint handling;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process; andi. guarantee of rights related to status as family members and community.

CHAPTER VI

SERVICES FOR VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 12

1. Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence, are as follows: a. complaint services;
b. medical services; and c. medicolegal services for the purposes of evidence in the field of law. (2) Legal aid services: a. independence or empowerment services; and b. social rehabilitation services.
3. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), are carried out by relevant SKPD in accordance with the programs, activities, and budgets available in each in the fields of: a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection;
d. mental and spiritual;
e. manpower; and f. education.

Article 5

Children who are victims of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in article 4, also receive the following special rights: a. the right to survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic service needs;
c. the right to equal protection; andd. the right to obtain freedom;

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 6

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection to women and children from acts of violence are the shared responsibility of: a. regional government;
b. community;
c. family;andd. parents.

